  "answer": "I'll start by exploring the workspace to understand the project structure.\nBun is available. Now applying the two fixes:\nBoth defects are fixed and the existing test verifies the repair.\n\n## Changes\n\n**`src/config.ts`** — parsing was too permissive (`Boolean(value)` coerced `'false'`, `1`, `[]`, `{}` all to enabled):\n```ts\nexport function enabled(value: unknown) { return value === true }\n```\nOnly the literal boolean `true` now enables the feature; every other value is disabled.\n\n**`src/display.ts`** — the false branch rendered the enabled string:\n```ts\nexport function display(value: boolean) { return value ? 'ON' : 'OFF' }\n```\n\nAPIs are unchanged: same module paths, names, arity, `unknown`/`boolean` parameter types, and `boolean`/`string` return shapes. No files were added, `package.json` and `test/oracle.test.ts` were left untouched, and the test runner was not replaced.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `bun test` → **1 pass, 0 fail**, 6 `expect()` calls: `false`, `'false'`, `1`, `null`, `undefined` all reach `'OFF'`, and `true` reaches `'ON'`.\n- Extra ad-hoc check (temp file, since removed) confirmed `'true'`, `0`, `NaN`, `[]`, `{}` are all treated as disabled and that `display` maps both states correctly — `ON`/`OFF`.\n- Workspace listing confirms only the two source files changed and no scratch files remain."
